{
 "id": "2021-yang-traditional",
 "name": "Traditional Village Landscapes: Values and Preservation (Qing Mu Chuan)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Yang, Qiu & Fu (2021), \"Toward Cultural Heritage Sustainability through Participatory Planning Based on Investigation of the Value Perceptions and Preservation Attitudes: Qing Mu Chuan, China\", Sustainability 13:1171. Participants say whether they are native residents of Qing Mu Chuan or landscape professionals, then see the paper's nine landscape scenes of the traditional village (three natural, two agricultural, four cultural) one at a time. For each scene they rate preference, four value perceptions (\"This landscape is essential to daily life\", \"The landscape can create economic income in the future\", \"The landscape has a recreational function, where I am relaxed\", \"The landscape is a part of the traditional village heritage and has cultural significance\"; 1 not very highly … 5 very highly) and preservation willingness, all on 5-point scales. Analysis as in the paper: ANOVA with post-hoc tests across scenes, independent t-tests residents vs professionals, stepwise regression of preservation willingness on the four values. Photographs: the paper's own (Appendix A, CC BY 4.0), used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) online self-paced pages instead of a 60-second iPad slideshow (residents) or an online meeting (professionals); (2) the screening question for natives becomes a group question with a \"neither\" option; (3) the paper does not report the slide order or the second-part questions on conservation planning, tourism, ecology and participation, so scenes follow the Appendix A order and the second part is not included; (4) the end labels of the preference and preservation scales are ours; (5) English instead of Chinese.",
